# Local development values. `pnpm run setup` copies this file to .dev.vars (git-ignored).
# Everything below works with `pnpm dev` as is. In production, set your own values in the dashboard
# (Worker > Settings > Variables and Secrets) or with `wrangler secret put <NAME>`.

# Key-encryption key for stored credentials: 32 bytes, base64.
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY: this value is public (base64 of "DEV-ONLY-KEY-NOT-FOR-PRODUCTION!"). The Worker
# refuses it unless AUTH_ACCESS_DEVELOPMENT is set and no Access variable is set.
# Production: generate your own OUTSIDE Cloudflare (`openssl rand -base64 32`) and keep a copy in
# your company vault. Losing it means losing every stored credential. Rotation: TOKENGATE_KEYRING
# (docs/operations/key-rotation.md).
TOKENGATE_KEK=REVWLU9OTFktS0VZLU5PVC1GT1ItUFJPRFVDVElPTiE=

# Development login: every admin request to localhost, 127.0.0.1 or [::1] is signed in as this
# email, with no check at all. Never set it in production. It cannot be combined with the Access
# variables below: if both are set, no login works.
AUTH_ACCESS_DEVELOPMENT=dev@example.com

# Production login (Cloudflare Access): "<team>" or "https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com", and the
# application AUD. Remove AUTH_ACCESS_DEVELOPMENT when you set them.
# AUTH_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
# AUTH_ACCESS_AUD=

# Stored credentials are bound to the three AUTH_ACCESS_* values: changing, adding or removing any of
# them makes every stored credential impossible to decrypt. Set them once, before you add services.
